export const body = "<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>A game of three</li><li>Transparent communication</li><li>Understanding Client B</li><li>Understanding the setting</li><li>In praise of Client A</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Some tips for producers</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>In conclusion</li></ul>\n\n<p>One of the key lessons we learned from the project is to treat a client’s project like our own. Today, we will expand on this lesson with the story about a<em></em> game—one we made in collaboration with two different clients.</p>\n\n\n<h3>A game of three</h3>\n\n<p>This project was especially difficult since we technically had to work with two clients, <strong>Client A </strong>and <strong>Client B</strong>. However, we only worked directly with Client A, who had to follow Client B's guidance. Our deliverables must meet the expectations of both of them, and their expectations were very different from each other. In short, our assets had to go through two stages of approval, making the wait for feedback became extra long.</p>\n\n<p>Due to this non-linear workflow, we encountered a number of problems that raised our efficiency to 300% at the beginning:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Unfinalized concept arts</li><li>Back-and-forth changes due to having technically two clients</li><li>Different requirements from each respective client</li></ul>\n\n<p>It was a rough start, but we managed to address the issues.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e3871bad99e77e9d2df4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e3871bad99e77e9d2df4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Transparent communication</h3>\n\n<p>Because communication sits at the heart of every project, the teams decided to tackle it first. They had two primary channels for this project.</p>\n\n<p>For client:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Use Slack to discuss minor issues</li><li>Hold weekly meeting with the stakeholders to set specific goals; ADS would elaborate or clarify on the quests if necessary</li><li>Separate the feedback, but inform everyone of potential changes and agreements</li></ul>\n\n<p>For internal teams:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Hold meetings for new character requests to keep the character and animation teams on the same page</li><li>Allow the teams to analyze and find solutions together</li></ul>\n\n<p>The next blocker to remove was the indirect feedback from Client B. The chart below illustrates the feedback loop across three entities in this project.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6435c2b21f4e317dacf9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6435c2b21f4e317dacf9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"495\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding Client B</h3>\n\n<p>Dealing with one client is hard, two even more so. Therefore, instead of waiting for Client A to send the feedback, the team started to pick up a pattern after the first three deliveries, and decided to study the feedback together. Below are some specific measures they had taken to address Client B's feedback:1</p>\n\n<p>Revisit old feedback files to identify spec-related issues to create a checklist for future assets2</p>\n\n<p>Analyze the feedback to define the art direction, and encourage all artists to propose their ideas and suggestions3</p>\n\n<p>Find the logic behind their feedback: the character’s function in the game, specific gameplay requirements, etc4</p>\n\n<p>Bring up production-related questions to the client timely to reduce misinformation5</p>\n\n<p>Divide the revision phase into multiple stages to track which stage often received the most feedback</p>\n\n<p>Eventually, the team came to a conclusion: Client A prioritizes art quality while Client B prioritizes gameplay and <em>fidelity to the setti</em>ng. This realization allowed them to propose their ideas to Client A based on Client B’s expectations. And Client A was open to cooperating with the team this way.</p>\n\n<p>As we mentioned before, all assets had to be <strong>approved by Client A before being sent to Client B</strong>. If Client B gave some feedback on said assets, Client A would pass the word to the team. The team would address the feedback accordingly, but depending on the content of the feedback, they might need to revise the assets from an earlier stage.</p>\n\n<p>For example, if Client B requires some revision on the high-poly model after the textures have been approved by Client A, the team would have to rework not only the high-poly stage, but also the low-poly stage, baking and texturing—very time-consuming. Client A would then be charged for this rework since they approved the assets before.</p>\n\n<p>To avoid that time-consuming process, the team would<strong> let Client A know the extra time spent and the causes of the reworks</strong> to get rid of them in the future. Client A would pay close attention to those causes and help pass the message to Client B. Additionally, Client A would also elaborate on B's feedback and provide suggestions with paintovers.</p>\n\n<p>\"It is more about teamworking rather than pointing out who is responsible for the additional revision,\".</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1c0a6376b2c17eaa95e4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1c0a6376b2c17eaa95e4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"478\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Understanding the setting</h3>\n\n<p>To understand Client B is to understand <em>the project</em>. Studying the IP was integral to this project. Not only did the team watch all the movies, but they also read books and wiki pages (such as the project Encyclopedia) to gain deeper knowledge.</p>\n\n<p>The goal was to understand the universe and characters in order to identify new characters just from their looks and weapons of choice. We want to tell which planet a character comes from just by his or her appearance.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>A Jedi droid</strong></p>\n<p>There was a character whose description reads: “A robot but dreaming of becoming a Jedi, so he dresses like a Jedi and acts like one.” This is one of first characters of the project, and since there were no Jedi-like droids in the movies, it was a challenge for both the Character team and the Animation Team.</p>\n<p>The Character Team had to capture the looks and feels of a Jedi in the droid while maintaining his mechanical features. They needed to avoid making him feel clumsy and emotionless. After a lot of extensive research and iterative efforts, the teams finally found the correct balance the client was asking for.</p>\n<p><em>Note</em><em>: picture is just for illustration purposes.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/559df0edf4974e09a1f0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/559df0edf4974e09a1f0.webp\" width=\"1065\" height=\"860\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>In praise of Client A</h3>\n\n<p>Working with clients has always been difficult. Clients can be extremely busy with their internal development; sometimes they have to work with multiple external studios; therefore, the time they spend with us can be limited, and the communication can be lacking. Some common obstacles arising from the client side include:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Feedback quality (too vague or general)</li><li>Feedback frequency (few and far between)</li><li>Long, unintended downtime (waiting for approval)</li><li>Strict deadlines</li><li>Etc.</li></ul>\n\n<p>Clients, of course, expect us to deliver high quality products, but they just don't always have the capacity to lend us a hand. They wasted no time.</p>\n\n<ul><li>We get feedback just 1 day after delivery</li><li>We never have to stay idle while waiting for feedback thanks to their well-planned backlog</li><li>We always know what to focus thanks to their clear priority</li><li>We always have enough time to address Client B’s feedback because they perform internal estimation for spec change</li></ul>\n\n<h4>Some tips for producers</h4>\n\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><ul><li>Document all the conversations with the client (e-mail, IM, Skype chat, etc.)</li><li>Don’t be afraid to disagree with the client; alert the client if their feedback or request doesn't align with the original documentation</li><li>Directly address the client’s concerns, never downplay or ignore them</li><li>Always ask questions if you don’t completely understand what the client is asking for</li><li>Respond to their emails regardless of who on the client side sent them</li><li>Send a recap email to the client after a meeting to ensure nothing was missed from your side</li><li>Be open about the project status to everyone: what is going on and how you feel about the situation</li></ul></div>\n\n<h3>In conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>Working on an <em>established</em> IP is always a daunting task, but with a well-coordinated workflow and a sensible approach, we can do it. This endeavor shows how we can be more than just another vendor on the market: we need to put ourselves in the client’s shoes and treat the project as our own. We hope this sharing can inspire other teams to seamlessly collaborate with their clients.</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading, and happy creating!</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bd74e8c814b472064a8e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bd74e8c814b472064a8e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"304\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/1c0a6376b2c17eaa95e4.webp", "assets/library-inline/559df0edf4974e09a1f0.webp", "assets/library-inline/6435c2b21f4e317dacf9.webp", "assets/library-inline/bd74e8c814b472064a8e.webp", "assets/library-inline/e3871bad99e77e9d2df4.webp"];
